<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bantham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bantham is a coastal site at the mouth of the River Avon in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Finds of imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> and fine wares show that it was a trading and landing place in the 5th and 6th centuries, probably linked to the export of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> to the Byzantine world. It is one of the main archaeological sites of post-Roman Dumnonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bantham.jpg" alt="Bantham" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark.murphy (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bantham_devon_village.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
